1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:06,733
A link budget is the answer to the question every radio listener eventually asks:

2
00:00:06,733 --> 00:00:10,706
that transmitter is way across the county — will I actually hear it?

3
00:00:10,706 --> 00:00:11,641
You could guess.

4
00:00:11,641 --> 00:00:15,439
You could drive around with an antenna and find out the hard way.

5
00:00:15,439 --> 00:00:20,289
Or you could do what every radio engineer on Earth does: follow the signal from the

6
00:00:20,289 --> 00:00:24,963
transmitter's amplifier all the way to your receiver's input, writing down every

7
00:00:24,963 --> 00:00:29,638
single gain and every single loss along the way — and know the answer before you

8
00:00:29,638 --> 00:00:30,631
plug anything in.

9
00:00:31,081 --> 00:00:36,409
The trick that makes this bookkeeping possible is the decibel — a logarithmic ratio

10
00:00:36,409 --> 00:00:41,738
scale on which every multiplication becomes simple addition and plus three dB means

11
00:00:41,738 --> 00:00:42,636
doubled power.

12
00:00:42,636 --> 00:00:47,772
So the whole journey — amplifiers multiplying, cables eating fractions, the path

13
00:00:47,772 --> 00:00:53,036
dividing the signal by billions — collapses into one running sum of small numbers.

14
00:00:53,036 --> 00:00:57,915
A link budget is exactly that sum, and it fits on one line of a spreadsheet.

15
00:00:58,365 --> 00:00:59,389
Let's walk one.

16
00:00:59,389 --> 00:01:02,734
The transmitter makes plus forty dBm — ten watts.

17
00:01:02,734 --> 00:01:07,170
Its feedline and connectors eat two dB on the climb up the tower.

18
00:01:07,170 --> 00:01:12,289
The antenna adds nine dB of gain by focusing the energy toward the horizon.

19
00:01:12,289 --> 00:01:17,750
Forty, minus two, plus nine: the signal leaves the site at an effective radiated

20
00:01:17,750 --> 00:01:19,456
power of forty-seven dBm.

21
00:01:19,456 --> 00:01:25,122
That launch figure has a name — EIRP — and it's the first line of every budget ever

22
00:01:25,122 --> 00:01:25,668
written.

23
00:01:26,118 --> 00:01:28,999
Then comes the path — and the path is brutal.

24
00:01:28,999 --> 00:01:34,312
Spreading out through space, the signal sheds more decibels than everything else in

25
00:01:34,312 --> 00:01:39,306
the chain combined; over a clear line of sight that loss is computed from just

26
00:01:39,306 --> 00:01:44,427
distance and frequency by the Friis transmission equation, and terrain, foliage,

27
00:01:44,427 --> 00:01:49,037
rain, and polarization mismatch each pile their own subtractions on top.

28
00:01:49,037 --> 00:01:51,982
On the staircase chart, the path isn't a step.

29
00:01:51,982 --> 00:01:52,942
It's the cliff.

30
00:01:53,392 --> 00:01:58,733
At your end, you claw a little back: your antenna's gain, minus your own feedline

31
00:01:58,733 --> 00:01:59,063
loss.

32
00:01:59,063 --> 00:02:03,415
Where the staircase finally lands is the predicted received power.

33
00:02:03,415 --> 00:02:04,865
Now comes the verdict.

34
00:02:04,865 --> 00:02:10,272
Compare it against your receiver's sensitivity — the minimum power it needs for an

35
00:02:10,272 --> 00:02:14,163
acceptable error rate — and the surplus is the link margin.

36
00:02:14,163 --> 00:02:16,669
Comfortably positive: the link closes.

37
00:02:16,669 --> 00:02:18,581
Thin or negative: unreliable.

38
00:02:18,581 --> 00:02:24,054
And a robust design never aims for zero — it reserves a fade margin on top, so that

39
00:02:24,054 --> 00:02:29,329
fading, multipath, and weather can't drag the link below threshold on a bad day.

40
00:02:29,779 --> 00:02:34,804
What makes the budget more than arithmetic is that it runs in both directions.

41
00:02:34,804 --> 00:02:38,798
Forward, it predicts coverage from equipment you already have.

42
00:02:38,798 --> 00:02:43,758
Backward, it solves for the missing piece: how much antenna gain, or how much

43
00:02:43,758 --> 00:02:47,688
transmit power, do I need for ten dB of margin at this range?

44
00:02:47,688 --> 00:02:52,841
Every term is a straight addition, so the design question and its answer live on

45
00:02:52,841 --> 00:02:53,743
the same line.

46
00:02:54,193 --> 00:02:57,087
And here's the bottom line of a budget, live.

47
00:02:57,087 --> 00:03:00,496
This is GopherTrunk locked to a real control channel.

48
00:03:00,496 --> 00:03:05,705
The signal on this waterfall left a transmitter at full power, lost almost all of

49
00:03:05,705 --> 00:03:11,043
itself to the path, and arrived here: the dBFS meter shows exactly what's left, and

50
00:03:11,043 --> 00:03:16,060
the SNR readout is the margin story in real time — how far the survivor stands

51
00:03:16,060 --> 00:03:17,025
above my noise.

52
00:03:17,025 --> 00:03:22,427
Because it clears what the demodulator needs, the decode counter just keeps ticking.

53
00:03:22,427 --> 00:03:25,964
Every one of those frames is a link budget that closed.

54
00:03:26,414 --> 00:03:30,619
And when a channel doesn't decode, the budget is your diagnosis.

55
00:03:30,619 --> 00:03:36,006
A negative margin isn't "bad reception" — it's a number: exactly how many dB short

56
00:03:36,006 --> 00:03:36,531
you are.

57
00:03:36,531 --> 00:03:41,721
Each fix has a dB value of its own — a better antenna, a lower-loss feedline, a

58
00:03:41,721 --> 00:03:46,911
quieter front end, a better location — so you can pick the cheapest combination

59
00:03:46,911 --> 00:03:49,736
that covers the gap before spending a cent.

60
00:03:50,186 --> 00:03:55,489
So: a link budget follows a signal from transmitter to receiver, adding every gain

61
00:03:55,489 --> 00:04:00,663
and subtracting every loss in decibels; received power minus sensitivity is your

62
00:04:00,663 --> 00:04:05,773
margin, with a fade reserve on top; and when a channel won't decode, the budget

63
00:04:05,773 --> 00:04:10,041
tells you how many dB you're short — and which fix buys them back.

64
00:04:10,041 --> 00:04:11,787
Full write-up linked below.

